WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:08.020
Continuous recording preserves full timelines but consumes substantial storage and retains unrelated activity.

2
00:00:08.120 --> 00:00:14.680
Event-based storage keeps clips, thumbnails, and metadata around detected or reviewed events.

3
00:00:14.780 --> 00:00:20.460
Many systems combine a shorter continuous window with longer retention for confirmed events.

4
00:00:20.740 --> 00:00:27.140
An NVR may remain the authoritative video store while the AI system holds event references and metadata.

5
00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:29.480
Avoid unnecessary duplicate copies.

6
00:00:29.580 --> 00:00:38.860
If AI clips are separate, record camera, start and end time, original source, model version, and review status so evidence can be traced.

7
00:00:39.140 --> 00:00:42.340
Metadata is smaller than video but still sensitive.

8
00:00:42.440 --> 00:00:49.960
Object labels, descriptions, zones, timestamps, alerts, and reviewer notes can reveal routines and operations.

9
00:00:50.060 --> 00:00:55.740
Apply the same purpose, access, and deletion principles used for footage.

10
00:00:56.020 --> 00:01:04.420
Retention policies define how long continuous video, event clips, thumbnails, metadata, exports, and audit logs remain.

11
00:01:04.520 --> 00:01:09.560
Set periods by legitimate need and applicable policy, not simply by available disk.

12
00:01:09.660 --> 00:01:13.660
Confirm that deletion includes secondary indexes and copies.

13
00:01:13.940 --> 00:01:22.020
Search and retrieval need reliable camera identity, synchronized time, event type, zone, and review outcome.

14
00:01:22.120 --> 00:01:28.840
Indexes should help authorized users find relevant events without encouraging broad browsing unrelated to the stated purpose.

15
00:01:29.120 --> 00:01:39.520
Protect storage with access control, encryption where appropriate, integrity monitoring, capacity alerts, and tested recovery for configuration and critical event records.

16
00:01:39.620 --> 00:01:42.740
Plan for disk failure and full volumes.

17
00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:50.440
A storage design is successful when it supports necessary review while minimizing unnecessary retention and exposure.
